# Cursor Rules for Next.js 15.3 Blog/Portfolio

## Project Context
You are working on a modern Next.js 15.3 blog/portfolio application with the following stack:
- **Framework**: Next.js 15.3.4 with App Router
- **Language**: TypeScript (strict mode)
- **Styling**: Tailwind CSS v4 + shadcn/ui components
- **React**: Version 19 (latest features)
- **Deployment**: Vercel
- **Dev Server**: Turbopack enabled for fast refresh

## Important: Development Workflow
- **DO NOT** manually start the development server or run npm commands
- The project uses `./start.sh` which handles:
  - Auto-restarting on file changes
  - Port management (kills existing processes on port 3000)
  - Dependency installation
  - Environment setup
  - Browser opening
- Files auto-refresh when saved, no need to restart servers

## Code Style & Conventions

### TypeScript
- Use TypeScript strict mode
- Prefer `interface` over `type` for object shapes
- Use `const` assertions for literal types
- Always define explicit return types for functions
- Use proper type imports: `import type { ... }`

### React & Next.js Patterns
```typescript
// Use modern Next.js 15 patterns
export default async function Page() {
  // Server Components by default
  const data = await fetchData()
  return <ClientComponent initialData={data} />
}

// Client Components only when needed
'use client'
export function ClientComponent({ initialData }) {
  // Interactive features here
}
```

### Component Structure
- Server Components by default
- Client Components only for:
  - useState, useEffect, other hooks
  - onClick, onChange handlers
  - Browser-only APIs
  - Third-party client libraries

### File Organization
```
frontend/
├── app/
│   ├── (routes)/           # Route groups
│   ├── api/                # API routes
│   ├── layout.tsx          # Root layout
│   └── page.tsx            # Home page
├── components/
│   ├── ui/                 # shadcn/ui components
│   └── [feature]/          # Feature components
├── lib/
│   ├── utils.ts            # Utility functions
│   └── types.ts            # Shared types
├── public/                 # Static assets
└── content/                # MDX blog posts (if using)
```

## UI Development with shadcn/ui

### Installing Components
```bash
# Use npx shadcn@latest add [component]
npx shadcn@latest add button card form
```

### Component Usage
```typescript
import { Button } from "@/components/ui/button"
import { Card, CardContent, CardDescription, CardHeader, CardTitle } from "@/components/ui/card"

// Always use the shadcn/ui design system
<Button variant="default" size="lg">
  Click me
</Button>
```

### Tailwind CSS v4
- Use Tailwind CSS v4 syntax
- Prefer semantic color tokens: `bg-background`, `text-foreground`
- Use CSS variables for theming
- Dark mode support built-in

## Database Considerations

For a blog/portfolio, you have several options:

### Option 1: No Database (Recommended for simple blogs)
```typescript
// Use MDX files in content directory
import { getAllPosts } from '@/lib/mdx'

export default async function BlogPage() {
  const posts = await getAllPosts()
  return <PostList posts={posts} />
}
```

### Option 2: Vercel KV (For view counts, likes)
```typescript
import { kv } from '@vercel/kv'

export async function incrementViews(slug: string) {
  await kv.incr(`views:${slug}`)
}
```

### Option 3: Vercel Postgres (For comments, contact forms)
```typescript
import { sql } from '@vercel/postgres'

export async function getComments(postId: string) {
  const { rows } = await sql`
    SELECT * FROM comments WHERE post_id = ${postId}
  `
  return rows
}
```

## Performance Best Practices

### Image Optimization
```typescript
import Image from 'next/image'

// Always use Next.js Image component
<Image
  src="/hero.jpg"
  alt="Hero image"
  width={1200}
  height={600}
  priority // for above-fold images
/>
```

### Font Optimization
```typescript
import { Inter, Playfair_Display } from 'next/font/google'

const inter = Inter({ 
  subsets: ['latin'],
  variable: '--font-inter'
})
```

### Static Generation
```typescript
// Prefer static generation for blog posts
export async function generateStaticParams() {
  const posts = await getAllPosts()
  return posts.map((post) => ({
    slug: post.slug,
  }))
}
```

## SEO & Metadata

### Use Next.js 15 Metadata API
```typescript
import type { Metadata } from 'next'

export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: 'Your Name - Portfolio',
  description: 'Full-stack developer specializing in...',
  openGraph: {
    title: 'Your Name - Portfolio',
    description: 'Full-stack developer specializing in...',
    url: 'https://yoursite.com',
    siteName: 'Your Portfolio',
    images: ['/og-image.jpg'],
    locale: 'en_US',
    type: 'website',
  },
  twitter: {
    card: 'summary_large_image',
    title: 'Your Name - Portfolio',
    description: 'Full-stack developer specializing in...',
    images: ['/twitter-image.jpg'],
  },
}
```

### Dynamic Metadata
```typescript
export async function generateMetadata({ params }): Promise<Metadata> {
  const post = await getPost(params.slug)
  return {
    title: post.title,
    description: post.excerpt,
  }
}
```

## Deployment to Vercel

### Environment Variables
```env
# .env.local
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://yourdomain.com
NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_ID=G-XXXXXXXXXX

# .env.production (in Vercel dashboard)
DATABASE_URL=postgres://...
KV_REST_API_URL=https://...
```

### Build Configuration
```json
// vercel.json
{
  "buildCommand": "npm run build",
  "outputDirectory": ".next",
  "devCommand": "npm run dev",
  "installCommand": "npm install"
}
```

## Common Patterns

### Blog Post List
```typescript
// app/blog/page.tsx
import { getPosts } from '@/lib/posts'
import { PostCard } from '@/components/post-card'

export default async function BlogPage() {
  const posts = await getPosts()
  
  return (
    <div className="container py-10">
      <h1 className="text-4xl font-bold mb-8">Blog</h1>
      <div className="grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
        {posts.map((post) => (
          <PostCard key={post.slug} post={post} />
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  )
}
```

### Contact Form (Server Actions)
```typescript
// app/contact/actions.ts
'use server'

import { z } from 'zod'
import { resend } from '@/lib/resend'

const schema = z.object({
  name: z.string().min(2),
  email: z.string().email(),
  message: z.string().min(10)
})

export async function sendMessage(formData: FormData) {
  const parsed = schema.parse({
    name: formData.get('name'),
    email: formData.get('email'),
    message: formData.get('message')
  })
  
  await resend.emails.send({
    from: 'contact@yoursite.com',
    to: 'you@email.com',
    subject: `Contact from ${parsed.name}`,
    text: parsed.message
  })
}
```

## Error Handling

### Error Boundaries
```typescript
// app/error.tsx
'use client'

export default function Error({
  error,
  reset,
}: {
  error: Error & { digest?: string }
  reset: () => void
}) {
  return (
    <div className="flex flex-col items-center justify-center min-h-[400px]">
      <h2 className="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Something went wrong!</h2>
      <Button onClick={() => reset()}>Try again</Button>
    </div>
  )
}
```

### Loading States
```typescript
// app/blog/loading.tsx
import { Skeleton } from "@/components/ui/skeleton"

export default function Loading() {
  return (
    <div className="container py-10">
      <Skeleton className="h-10 w-[200px] mb-8" />
      <div className="grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
        {Array.from({ length: 6 }).map((_, i) => (
          <Skeleton key={i} className="h-[300px]" />
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  )
}
```

## Testing Approach
- Unit tests for utilities: Vitest
- Component tests: React Testing Library
- E2E tests: Playwright
- Run tests before deployment

## Performance Monitoring
- Use Vercel Analytics for Core Web Vitals
- Monitor bundle size with `next-bundle-analyzer`
- Implement proper error tracking (Sentry)

## Remember
1. The dev server auto-restarts - no manual intervention needed
2. Always use Server Components unless client interactivity is required
3. Leverage Next.js 15's built-in optimizations
4. Use shadcn/ui components for consistent UI
5. Keep it simple - you might not need a database for a basic portfolio 